WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.960
How does union-find work? It

2
00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:04.440
keeps track of groups that

3
00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:06.540
do not overlap, such as

4
00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:07.820
which people are on the

5
00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:09.800
same team. We will use

6
00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:11.560
eight letters, starting with eight

7
00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:12.380
separate groups.

8
00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:14.600
Union means merge the groups

9
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:17.040
containing two elements. It first

10
00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.000
locates their roots, then makes

11
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.240
one root point to the

12
00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:22.080
other. Let us see the

13
00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.280
merge first; afterwards, we will

14
00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:25.900
learn how find locates a

15
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:26.140
root.

16
00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:28.660
Call union on A and

17
00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:30.540
B. Link root A under

18
00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:32.900
root B, so A now

19
00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:35.100
belongs to B's group. The

20
00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:36.840
parent table changes from A

21
00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:38.300
to B in A's column;

22
00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:40.560
B still points to itself.

23
00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:42.500
We use a score called

24
00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:43.760
rank to keep the trees

25
00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:46.120
shallow. Put a lower-rank root

26
00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:47.760
below a higher-rank root; on

27
00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:49.520
a tie, this course puts

28
00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:50.860
the first root below the

29
00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:52.520
second and increases the second

30
00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:55.080
root's rank. These choices change

31
00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:56.980
the shape, not which elements

32
00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:57.800
share a group.

33
00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:00.080
Now merge C with D.

34
00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:01.920
Put root C below root

35
00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:03.560
D, and raise D's rank

36
00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:05.560
to one. Every arrow points

37
00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:07.020
towards the root that represents

38
00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:07.500
the group.

39
00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.000
Now merge E with F.

40
00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:11.880
Put root E below root

41
00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:13.520
F, and raise F's rank

42
00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:15.660
to one. Every arrow points

43
00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:17.240
towards the root that represents

44
00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:17.740
the group.

45
00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:20.300
Now merge G with H.

46
00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:22.180
Put root G below root

47
00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:24.040
H, and raise H's rank

48
00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:26.240
to one. Every arrow points

49
00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:27.700
towards the root that represents

50
00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:28.160
the group.

51
00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:30.320
We now have four pairs:

52
00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:31.840
A with B, C with

53
00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:32.940
D, E with F, and

54
00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:35.317
G with H. Each coloured

55
00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:36.200
tree is one group. Union

56
00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:37.680
only changes a root's parent

57
00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:39.700
pointer; every descendant comes along.

58
00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:42.400
Next merge the groups containing

59
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:43.760
B and D. Link root

60
00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:45.620
B below root D, and

61
00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:47.380
increase D's rank to two.

62
00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:49.380
A still points to B,

63
00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:51.600
so A comes along automatically;

64
00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:53.600
we do not detach it.

65
00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:55.580
Merge the groups containing F

66
00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:56.420
and H in the same

67
00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:58.660
way. Link F below H,

68
00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:01.300
making H's rank two. E

69
00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:02.860
stays below F, and G

70
00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:04.760
stays below H; these four

71
00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:06.340
elements now share one group.

72
00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:08.740
Finally merge the two four-element

73
00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:10.780
groups. Link D below H

74
00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:12.300
and raise H's rank to

75
00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:14.500
three. All eight elements now

76
00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:16.180
share one root, even though

77
00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:18.040
several arrows still form longer

78
00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:18.380
paths.

79
00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:20.840
Find means tell me which

80
00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:22.600
group this element belongs to.

81
00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:24.540
Start at that element and

82
00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:26.220
follow its parent arrows until

83
00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:27.900
a vertex points to itself.

84
00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:30.360
That vertex is the representative;

85
00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:32.040
it need not be the

86
00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:32.820
smallest letter.

87
00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:34.960
Let us find the representative

88
00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:36.640
of A. Start at A

89
00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:37.920
and follow its parent to

90
00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:39.540
B. B is not a

91
00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:41.240
root because B points to

92
00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:42.980
D, so follow that arrow

93
00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:43.360
too.

94
00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:46.300
From D, follow the arrow

95
00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:48.340
to H. H is its

96
00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:50.440
own parent, so find stops

97
00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:53.080
and returns H. This only

98
00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:55.220
identifies A's group; it does

99
00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:56.580
not merge any groups.

100
00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:59.240
Path compression makes later finds

101
00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:01.960
faster. Change A's parent directly

102
00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:04.140
to H, and change B's

103
00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:06.340
parent directly to H; D

104
00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:07.601
already points there. We shortened

105
00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:10.340
the path, but all eight

106
00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:11.540
elements are still in the

107
00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:12.060
same group.

108
00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:15.060
Find A again. It now

109
00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:16.720
takes just one arrow to

110
00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:18.980
H instead of three. H's

111
00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:21.060
rank stays three after compression:

112
00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:23.140
rank is a historical upper

113
00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:24.880
bound on height, not the

114
00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:25.560
current height.

115
00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.040
Now let us combine both

116
00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:29.820
operations in a fresh example.

117
00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:31.780
We have reset to two

118
00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:34.200
groups: A, B, C, D

119
00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:36.200
under root D, and E,

120
00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:38.060
F, G, H under root

121
00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:40.340
H. Suppose we call union

122
00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:42.360
on A and E; neither

123
00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:43.360
argument is a root.

124
00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:46.480
First, find A by following

125
00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:48.600
A to B, then B

126
00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:49.820
to D. D is the

127
00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:51.080
root of the first group.

128
00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:52.580
Record D as the answer;

129
00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:55.100
a compressing find can also

130
00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:56.820
shorten A's parent pointer.

131
00:03:57.220 --> 00:03:59.520
Next, find E by following

132
00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:01.500
E to F, then F

133
00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:03.280
to H. H is the

134
00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:04.580
root of the second group.

135
00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:06.460
The roots are different, so

136
00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:08.180
union must merge these two

137
00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:08.480
groups.

138
00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:11.280
Both finds can also compress

139
00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:13.520
their paths. Point A directly

140
00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:15.420
to D, and E directly

141
00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:17.200
to H. The groups are

142
00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:19.320
still separate until union links

143
00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:19.820
their roots.

144
00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:23.120
After finding both roots, link

145
00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:25.040
D under H and increase

146
00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:27.300
H's rank to three. We

147
00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:28.420
link the roots, not A

148
00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:30.820
directly to E. This brings

149
00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:32.420
every element of the first

150
00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:34.220
group into the second group.

151
00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:36.020
If we now call union

152
00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:38.020
on B and G, both

153
00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:40.400
finds return H. Because the

154
00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:43.020
representatives match, do nothing: they

155
00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:45.280
already share a group. Never

156
00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:46.460
link a root back into

157
00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:47.200
its own tree.

158
00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:50.640
Why do these optimisations help?

159
00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:53.240
A rank increase requires merging

160
00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:55.620
two equally ranked groups, so

161
00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:57.020
rank r needs at least

162
00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:58.480
two to the power r

163
00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:00.820
elements. That limits rank to

164
00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:03.220
log n, giving logarithmic worst-case

165
00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:06.580
single operations; compression also makes

166
00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:08.620
repeated operations much faster.

167
00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:11.460
Across many operations, union by

168
00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:13.300
rank together with path compression

169
00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:15.847
gives inverse Ackermann n amortised

170
00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:17.960
time per operation. This function

171
00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:20.080
grows extremely slowly, but is

172
00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:22.740
not literally constant. Initialising n

173
00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:24.820
singletons and performing q more

174
00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:27.080
operations costs order n plus

175
00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:29.080
q times inverse Ackermann n.

176
00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:31.180
Things to take note of.

177
00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:33.640
Find may shorten parent paths,

178
00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:35.080
but it never merges groups;

179
00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:37.440
union merges only when roots

180
00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:40.020
differ. In Kruskal, different roots

181
00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:41.680
mean accept the graph edge

182
00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:43.780
and union the groups, while

183
00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:45.180
equal roots mean the edge

184
00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:46.000
would make a cycle.

185
00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:49.040
Union joins groups by linking

186
00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:50.960
their roots. Find follows parent

187
00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:52.810
arrows to report a root,

188
00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:54.300
and compression remembers a shorter

189
00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:56.680
route. Watch the parent table

190
00:05:56.680 --> 00:05:58.760
beside the trees: each changed

191
00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:00.540
arrow is one precise update,

192
00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:02.820
not a mysterious whole-tree operation.
